/* Pathology 6–9 */
Z("path6",0,"p",["TORCH infections are a group of infections a mother can pass to her baby in the womb, causing similar birth defects.","Like different burglars who all break the same window: different germs, same damage pattern in the fetus.",["T = Toxoplasma, O = Others (syphilis, varicella, parvovirus B19, HIV), R = Rubella, C = CMV, H = Herpes simplex.","Mother is often mildly ill or symptom-free.","Common features: growth restriction, microcephaly, hepatosplenomegaly, jaundice, rash, eye and hearing defects.","Diagnosis: IgM in the newborn (IgM does not cross placenta)."],"TORCH = same torch burns the same places: brain, eyes, ears, liver, skin."],
"TORCH complex is a group of transplacentally acquired infections producing overlapping fetal features: Toxoplasma, Others (syphilis, VZV, parvovirus B19, HIV, listeria), Rubella, CMV and Herpes simplex. Common findings are intrauterine growth restriction, microcephaly, intracranial calcification, chorioretinitis, cataract, hearing loss, hepatosplenomegaly, jaundice, thrombocytopenia and 'blueberry muffin' rash. Diagnosis rests on specific IgM in neonatal serum, PCR and maternal serology; screening in pregnancy allows counselling and prevention.",
[["Definition","Infections acquired by the fetus in utero (transplacental) or at birth, grouped because their clinical features overlap."],
["Agents and characteristic lesions",[["Agent","Key fetal lesion"],["Toxoplasma gondii","Chorioretinitis, hydrocephalus, diffuse intracranial calcification"],["Rubella","Cataract, PDA/pulmonary stenosis, sensorineural deafness (Gregg triad)"],["CMV (commonest)","Periventricular calcification, microcephaly, deafness, owl's-eye inclusions"],["HSV","Vesicles, encephalitis, keratoconjunctivitis (usually at birth)"],["Syphilis","Snuffles, Hutchinson teeth, saddle nose, osteochondritis"]]],
["Common features",["IUGR, prematurity","Microcephaly, seizures, developmental delay","Hepatosplenomegaly, jaundice, thrombocytopenia, purpura","Eye: chorioretinitis, cataract; ear: deafness"]],
["Diagnosis",["Neonatal IgM (does not cross placenta) — confirms fetal infection","PCR of amniotic fluid, urine (CMV), CSF","Maternal IgG avidity to date infection","Placental histology: villitis"]]],
{i:"The fetus is immunologically immature; infection during organogenesis (first trimester) causes malformations, while later infection produces inflammatory and destructive lesions.",x:[["Prevention",["Rubella vaccination (MMR) of girls before pregnancy","Avoid raw meat and cat litter (toxoplasma)","Antenatal VDRL screening and penicillin","Caesarean section for active genital herpes"]]],f:"Maternal infection → placentitis/villitis → fetal viraemia/parasitaemia\n   1st trimester → malformations (rubella, CMV)\n   Later → inflammation, necrosis, calcification",c:"TORCH infections are preventable causes of congenital disability; antenatal screening, vaccination and hygiene are the key measures."});

Z("path6",1,"p",["Both are forms of severe childhood malnutrition: kwashiorkor is mainly protein lack, marasmus is lack of overall calories.","Marasmus is an empty fuel tank; kwashiorkor is fuel present but no spare parts to repair the engine.",["Marasmus: skin and bones, no oedema, 'old man' face, alert.","Kwashiorkor: oedema, fatty liver, flaky-paint skin, flag sign in hair, apathy.","Kwashiorkor: low serum albumin → oedema.","Both: growth failure, infections, anaemia."],"K for Kwashiorkor = 'K'eeps water (oedema) and fat in liver."],
"Kwashiorkor results from protein deficiency with relatively adequate calories; marasmus from deficiency of total calories. Marasmus shows severe wasting of muscle and fat (weight < 60% of expected), no oedema, normal albumin and an alert, hungry child. Kwashiorkor shows pitting oedema, hypoalbuminaemia, fatty liver, flaky-paint dermatosis, hair depigmentation with flag sign, apathy and relatively preserved fat. Both have growth failure, immune deficiency, anaemia and vitamin deficiencies.",
[["Definition","Severe acute malnutrition: marasmus (calorie deficiency) and kwashiorkor (protein deficiency); mixed form = marasmic kwashiorkor."],
["Differences",[["Feature","Marasmus","Kwashiorkor"],["Deficiency","Calories (total energy)","Protein (calories may be adequate)"],["Age","< 1 year usually","1–4 years (after weaning)"],["Weight for age","< 60%","60–80%"],["Oedema","Absent","Present (pitting)"],["Serum albumin","Near normal","Markedly low"],["Liver","Normal","Enlarged, fatty"],["Skin / hair","Loose, wrinkled skin","Flaky-paint dermatosis, flag sign"],["Mood","Alert, hungry","Apathetic, irritable"],["Subcutaneous fat","Lost","Preserved"]]],
["Pathogenesis of oedema and fatty liver",["↓ Protein → ↓ albumin → ↓ oncotic pressure → oedema","↓ Apolipoprotein synthesis → fat cannot leave liver → fatty liver"]],
["Common morphology",["Small intestine villous atrophy (malabsorption)","Thymic and lymphoid atrophy → infections","Bone marrow hypoplasia → anaemia","Brain: reduced growth if early"]]],
{i:"Protein–energy malnutrition (PEM) remains a major cause of child mortality in developing countries; WHO classifies it by weight-for-height and oedema.",x:[["Management principles",["Stabilisation: treat hypoglycaemia, hypothermia, dehydration, infection","Rehabilitation with F-75 then F-100 / RUTF","Micronutrients (vitamin A, zinc, iron after stabilisation)"]]],c:"Oedema and hypoalbuminaemia separate kwashiorkor from marasmus; both reflect adaptive failure to dietary deficiency."});

Z("path6",2,"p",["Down's syndrome is caused by an extra chromosome 21.","Like a recipe with one ingredient added in triple instead of double: everything is slightly 'off'.",["Commonest chromosomal disorder in live births.","95% due to meiotic non-disjunction; risk rises with maternal age.","Features: flat face, upslanting eyes, single palmar crease, mental retardation.","Risks: heart defects (endocardial cushion), leukaemia, early Alzheimer's."],"21 = trisomy 21; 'three' copies, 'three' big risks: heart, leukaemia, Alzheimer."],
"Down's syndrome (trisomy 21) is the commonest chromosomal disorder. About 95% arise from meiotic non-disjunction (associated with advanced maternal age), 4% from Robertsonian translocation and 1% are mosaics. Features include intellectual disability, flat facial profile, oblique palpebral fissures, epicanthic folds, simian crease, hypotonia, congenital heart disease (endocardial cushion defects), duodenal atresia, 10–20× risk of acute leukaemia, early Alzheimer disease and immune defects. Prenatal diagnosis: triple test/NT scan, karyotype.",
[["Cytogenetics",["Trisomy 21 by non-disjunction (95%) — maternal age > 35","Robertsonian translocation t(14;21) (4%) — familial, not age related","Mosaicism (1%) — milder"]],
["Clinical features",["Intellectual disability, hypotonia","Flat face, epicanthic folds, upslanting palpebral fissures, Brushfield spots","Single palmar (simian) crease, gap between 1st and 2nd toes","Protruding tongue, small ears, short neck"]],
["Associations",["Congenital heart disease (40%): AV septal defect, VSD","GI: duodenal atresia, Hirschsprung disease","ALL and AML (esp. megakaryoblastic), transient myeloproliferative disorder","Early Alzheimer disease (APP gene on chr 21)","Hypothyroidism, infections"]],
["Diagnosis",["Antenatal: nuchal translucency, triple/quadruple test (↓AFP, ↑hCG, ↓estriol, ↑inhibin)","Karyotype by amniocentesis/CVS","Cell-free fetal DNA"]]],
{i:"Down syndrome affects about 1 in 700 live births and is the leading genetic cause of intellectual disability.",f:"Meiotic non-disjunction (maternal) → gamete with 2 copies of chr 21\n → fertilisation → 47,XX/XY,+21 → gene dosage effect → phenotype",c:"Trisomy 21 combines a typical facies with cardiac, haematological and neurological risks; antenatal screening allows early counselling."});

Z("path6",3,"p",["Klinefelter's syndrome is a male with an extra X chromosome (47,XXY).","A male body running with a female extra chromosome that switches down the testes.",["Tall, long legs, small firm testes.","Infertility (azoospermia), gynaecomastia.","Low testosterone, high FSH and LH.","Barr body present in a male."],"XXY = eXtra X in a guY → small testes, big breasts, tall."],
"Klinefelter syndrome is male hypogonadism due to two or more X chromosomes with at least one Y (commonly 47,XXY). It results from non-disjunction. Features appear at puberty: tall eunuchoid habitus with long legs, small atrophic testes, azoospermia and infertility, gynaecomastia, sparse body hair, and sometimes mild learning difficulty. Hormones: low testosterone, raised FSH and LH, raised oestradiol. Buccal smear shows Barr body; karyotype confirms. Risk of breast cancer, autoimmune disease and osteoporosis increases.",
[["Genetics","47,XXY (80%); variants 48,XXXY, mosaics 46,XY/47,XXY. Non-disjunction in either parent."],
["Clinical features",["Tall stature, eunuchoid body (lower segment > upper)","Small firm testes, azoospermia — commonest genetic cause of male infertility","Gynaecomastia, female-type hair and fat distribution","Reduced libido; mild IQ reduction"]],
["Pathology of testis",["Hyalinised seminiferous tubules (ghost tubules)","Prominent clusters of Leydig cells"]],
["Investigations",["↓ Testosterone, ↑ FSH, ↑ LH","Barr body positive buccal smear","Karyotype"]]],
{x:[["Associated risks",["Breast carcinoma (20× male risk)","Extragonadal germ cell tumours","SLE, osteoporosis, type 2 diabetes"]]],c:"Klinefelter syndrome should be suspected in any tall infertile male with small testes; testosterone replacement improves secondary sex characteristics."});

Z("path6",4,"p",["Turner's syndrome is a female with only one X chromosome (45,X).","A building with half the blueprints: short, with poorly built ovaries.",["Short stature, webbed neck, widely spaced nipples.","Streak ovaries → primary amenorrhoea, infertility.","Coarctation of aorta, bicuspid aortic valve.","Barr body absent."],"Turner = 'turned' only one X; short girl, webbed neck, no periods."],
"Turner syndrome results from complete or partial monosomy of the X chromosome (45,X in 57%, rest mosaics or structural X defects). It causes female hypogonadism. Features: short stature, webbed neck, low posterior hairline, shield chest, cubitus valgus, lymphoedema of hands and feet at birth (cystic hygroma in fetus), streak ovaries with primary amenorrhoea and infertility, coarctation of aorta, bicuspid aortic valve and horseshoe kidney. Intelligence is usually normal. Barr body absent; karyotype confirms.",
[["Genetics",["45,X (57%)","Mosaic 45,X/46,XX","Isochromosome Xq, ring X, deletion"]],
["Clinical features",["Short stature (SHOX gene loss)","Webbed neck, low hairline, shield chest, widely spaced nipples","Cubitus valgus, short 4th metacarpal","Lymphoedema of hands/feet; fetal cystic hygroma"]],
["Gonadal and systemic",["Streak ovaries → primary amenorrhoea, absent breasts","↑ FSH/LH, ↓ oestrogen","Coarctation of aorta, bicuspid aortic valve","Horseshoe kidney, hypothyroidism"]],
["Turner vs Klinefelter",[["Feature","Turner","Klinefelter"],["Karyotype","45,X","47,XXY"],["Sex","Female","Male"],["Height","Short","Tall"],["Barr body","Absent","Present"],["Gonad","Streak ovary","Atrophic testis"]]]],
{c:"Turner syndrome is the commonest sex chromosome abnormality in females; growth hormone and oestrogen replacement are the mainstays of treatment."});

Z("path6",5,"p",["Mendelian disorders are caused by a single faulty gene and follow Mendel's laws of inheritance.","Like a single wrong letter in a recipe that spoils the dish every time it is followed.",["Autosomal dominant: one bad copy enough; structural proteins (Marfan, ADPKD, FH).","Autosomal recessive: both copies bad; mostly enzymes (storage diseases, thalassaemia).","X-linked recessive: males affected (haemophilia, DMD, G6PD).","Vertical transmission in AD, horizontal in AR."],"Dominant = Doors/walls (structural); Recessive = Rooms' machinery (enzymes)."],
"Mendelian disorders result from mutation in a single gene of large effect and are inherited as autosomal dominant, autosomal recessive or X-linked. Autosomal dominant disorders (Marfan syndrome, familial hypercholesterolaemia, ADPKD, neurofibromatosis, achondroplasia) usually involve structural or receptor proteins, show vertical transmission, variable expressivity and incomplete penetrance. Autosomal recessive disorders (cystic fibrosis, sickle cell, thalassaemia, storage diseases, PKU) involve enzymes, are uniform and early onset. X-linked recessive disorders (haemophilia, DMD, G6PD deficiency) affect males via carrier mothers.",
[["Patterns",[["Feature","Autosomal dominant","Autosomal recessive","X-linked recessive"],["Affected","Both sexes, every generation","Siblings, both sexes","Males; females carriers"],["Risk to child","50%","25% (carrier parents)","50% of sons of carrier mother"],["Protein type","Structural/receptor","Enzymes","Various"],["Onset","Often adult","Early childhood","Childhood"],["Examples","Marfan, FH, ADPKD, NF1, Huntington","CF, PKU, sickle cell, Gaucher","Haemophilia A/B, DMD, G6PD"]]],
["Biochemical mechanisms",["Enzyme defects → substrate accumulation (storage disease) or product deficiency (albinism)","Receptor/transport defects (familial hypercholesterolaemia — LDL receptor; CF — CFTR)","Structural protein defects (Marfan — fibrillin-1; Ehlers–Danlos — collagen)","Growth regulators (NF1 — neurofibromin)"]],
["Key terms",["Penetrance: % of carriers expressing the trait","Expressivity: severity variation","Pleiotropy: one gene → many effects","Genetic heterogeneity: many genes → one phenotype"]]],
{c:"Recognising the inheritance pattern allows genetic counselling and carrier screening in affected families."});

Z("path6",6,"p",["Storage disorders occur when a missing enzyme lets material pile up inside lysosomes.","Like a house where the garbage collector never comes: rubbish fills every room.",["Mostly autosomal recessive enzyme defects.","Gaucher: glucocerebroside in macrophages — 'crumpled tissue paper' cells.","Niemann–Pick: sphingomyelin, foamy cells, cherry red spot.","Tay–Sachs: GM2 ganglioside in neurons, cherry red spot, no hepatosplenomegaly."],"Gaucher = Glucocerebrosidase; Niemann–Pick = sphingomyelinase; Tay–Sachs = hexosaminidase A."],
"Lysosomal storage disorders are inherited (mostly autosomal recessive) deficiencies of lysosomal enzymes causing accumulation of undigested substrate within lysosomes of cells, especially macrophages and neurons. Important examples: Gaucher disease (glucocerebrosidase deficiency; Gaucher cells with crumpled tissue-paper cytoplasm; hepatosplenomegaly, bone lesions), Niemann–Pick disease (sphingomyelinase; foamy macrophages, cherry red spot, neurodegeneration), Tay–Sachs (hexosaminidase A; GM2 ganglioside in neurons, cherry red spot, blindness), mucopolysaccharidoses (Hurler, Hunter) and glycogen storage diseases (von Gierke, Pompe, McArdle).",
[["Definition and mechanism","Deficiency of a lysosomal acid hydrolase → incompletely degraded substrate accumulates (primary storage) → lysosomes enlarge, cell function fails; macrophage-rich organs (liver, spleen, marrow) and neurons are hit."],
["Important lysosomal storage diseases",[["Disease","Enzyme deficient","Material stored","Key features"],["Gaucher (commonest)","Glucocerebrosidase","Glucocerebroside","Gaucher cells (wrinkled tissue paper), splenomegaly, bone pain, Erlenmeyer flask femur"],["Niemann–Pick A/B","Sphingomyelinase","Sphingomyelin","Foamy (zebra-body) macrophages, hepatosplenomegaly, cherry red spot"],["Tay–Sachs","Hexosaminidase A","GM2 ganglioside","Cherry red spot, neurodegeneration, Ashkenazi Jews"],["Hurler (MPS I)","α-L-iduronidase","Dermatan & heparan sulphate","Gargoylism, corneal clouding"],["Hunter (MPS II, X-linked)","Iduronate sulphatase","Same","No corneal clouding"],["Pompe (GSD II)","Acid maltase","Glycogen","Cardiomegaly, hypotonia"]]],
["Glycogen storage diseases",["Hepatic type — von Gierke (glucose-6-phosphatase): hepatomegaly, hypoglycaemia","Myopathic type — McArdle (muscle phosphorylase): cramps after exercise","Pompe — lysosomal, cardiac failure"]],
["Diagnosis",["Enzyme assay in leucocytes/fibroblasts","Marrow/organ biopsy showing storage cells","Molecular testing; prenatal diagnosis"]]],
{i:"Lysosomes contain acid hydrolases that degrade complex substrates; loss of one enzyme blocks a degradation pathway at a specific step.",x:[["Treatment",["Enzyme replacement therapy (imiglucerase for Gaucher)","Substrate reduction therapy (miglustat)","Haematopoietic stem cell transplantation"]]],f:"Mutant gene → ↓ lysosomal enzyme → undegraded substrate in lysosome\n → enlarged storage cells (macrophages, neurons)\n → hepatosplenomegaly / neurodegeneration / skeletal lesions",c:"Storage diseases share a common mechanism; the stored substrate and cell type explain the clinical picture, and enzyme replacement now treats several of them."});

Z("path7",0,"p",["Anaemia is a low haemoglobin level, so blood carries less oxygen. Iron deficiency gives small pale cells; B12/folate deficiency gives big cells.","Red cells are delivery trucks: in iron deficiency trucks are small and half-loaded; in megaloblastic anaemia trucks are huge but too few because the factory (DNA) is slow.",["Anaemia: Hb < 13 g/dL in men, < 12 in women.","IDA: microcytic hypochromic, low ferritin, high TIBC; commonest cause worldwide.","Megaloblastic: macrocytic, hypersegmented neutrophils; B12 or folate deficiency.","B12 deficiency also damages nerves (subacute combined degeneration)."],"Iron = small & pale (low ferritin); B12/folate = big & bad DNA (hypersegmented neutrophils)."],
"Anaemia is reduction of haemoglobin or red cell mass below normal for age and sex (Hb < 13 g/dL in men, < 12 g/dL in women), reducing oxygen-carrying capacity. It is classified by morphology (microcytic, normocytic, macrocytic) or cause (blood loss, decreased production, increased destruction). Iron deficiency anaemia, commonest worldwide, follows chronic blood loss, poor intake or increased demand; it produces microcytic hypochromic red cells, low ferritin and serum iron, high TIBC, koilonychia and pica. Megaloblastic anaemia from vitamin B12 or folate deficiency impairs DNA synthesis: macro-ovalocytes, hypersegmented neutrophils, megaloblastic marrow; B12 deficiency adds neurological damage.",
[["Definition and classification",["Morphological: microcytic hypochromic (IDA, thalassaemia, sideroblastic, ACD); normocytic normochromic (acute blood loss, haemolysis, aplastic, CKD); macrocytic (megaloblastic, liver disease, hypothyroidism)","Etiological: blood loss; impaired production (nutritional, marrow failure); increased destruction (haemolytic)"]],
["Iron deficiency anaemia",["Causes: chronic blood loss (hookworm, menorrhagia, GI cancer, peptic ulcer), dietary lack, increased demand (pregnancy, growth), malabsorption","Clinical: pallor, fatigue, koilonychia, angular stomatitis, glossitis, pica, Plummer–Vinson syndrome","Blood: microcytic hypochromic, pencil cells, anisopoikilocytosis; ↓MCV, ↓MCH, ↑RDW","Iron studies: ↓ ferritin (best test), ↓ serum iron, ↑ TIBC, ↓ transferrin saturation","Marrow: absent stainable iron, micronormoblasts"]],
["Megaloblastic anaemia",["Causes B12: pernicious anaemia (anti-intrinsic factor), gastrectomy, ileal disease, fish tapeworm, strict vegetarians","Causes folate: poor diet, pregnancy, alcoholism, methotrexate/phenytoin","Clinical: pallor, lemon-yellow tint, glossitis; B12 only — subacute combined degeneration, peripheral neuropathy","Blood: macro-ovalocytes (MCV > 100), hypersegmented neutrophils (> 5 lobes), pancytopenia","Marrow: hypercellular, megaloblasts (nuclear–cytoplasmic asynchrony), giant metamyelocytes","Biochem: ↓ B12/folate, ↑ methylmalonic acid (B12), ↑ homocysteine (both), ↑ LDH, indirect bilirubin"]],
["IDA vs megaloblastic",[["Feature","Iron deficiency","Megaloblastic"],["MCV","< 80 fL","> 100 fL"],["Smear","Microcytic, pencil cells","Macro-ovalocytes, hypersegmented PMN"],["Marrow","Micronormoblasts, no iron","Megaloblasts, giant metamyelocytes"],["Key test","Low ferritin","Low B12/folate"],["Neuro signs","No","Yes in B12"]]]],
{i:"Haemoglobin delivers oxygen; when it falls, tissues become hypoxic and compensatory mechanisms (tachycardia, increased 2,3-BPG, erythropoietin) appear. The clinical picture depends on speed of onset and cause.",x:[["Approach to an anaemic patient",["CBC with indices → MCV guides category","Peripheral smear and reticulocyte count (high = loss/haemolysis, low = production defect)","Specific tests: iron studies, B12/folate, haemolysis screen, marrow"]],["Treatment",["IDA: treat cause + oral ferrous sulphate (parenteral if intolerant/malabsorption)","Megaloblastic: hydroxocobalamin IM (pernicious anaemia lifelong) and folic acid; never give folate alone in B12 deficiency"]]],f:"Anaemia\n ├ Microcytic → ferritin ↓ = IDA | normal/↑ = thalassaemia, ACD, sideroblastic\n ├ Normocytic → retic ↑ = haemolysis/bleeding | retic ↓ = aplasia, CKD\n └ Macrocytic → megaloblastic (B12/folate) | non-megaloblastic (liver, alcohol, hypothyroid)",c:"MCV and the peripheral smear quickly direct the work-up; iron deficiency and megaloblastic anaemia are common, easily diagnosed and fully treatable once the cause is found."});

Z("path7",1,"p",["Haemolytic anaemia happens when red cells are destroyed faster than the marrow can replace them.","Like a factory working overtime because a vandal keeps smashing finished cars.",["Intrinsic (defect in RBC): spherocytosis, G6PD, sickle cell, thalassaemia, PNH.","Extrinsic (outside RBC): autoimmune, mechanical, infections (malaria), hypersplenism.","Lab: ↑ reticulocytes, ↑ indirect bilirubin, ↑ LDH, ↓ haptoglobin.","Intravascular haemolysis: haemoglobinuria, haemosiderinuria."],"Haemolysis = 'more young cells (retics) + more yellow (bilirubin) + less haptoglobin'."],
"Haemolytic anaemias are anaemias due to shortened red cell survival (< 120 days) with compensatory marrow hyperplasia. They are classified as intracorpuscular (hereditary — membrane defects such as spherocytosis, enzyme defects such as G6PD and pyruvate kinase deficiency, haemoglobinopathies like sickle cell and thalassaemia; acquired — PNH) and extracorpuscular (immune — autoimmune, alloimmune, drug-induced; non-immune — mechanical/MAHA, infections like malaria, hypersplenism, burns). Laboratory evidence: anaemia, reticulocytosis, unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia, raised LDH, low haptoglobin, urobilinogen in urine; DCT for immune cause.",
[["Classification",[["Intracorpuscular","Extracorpuscular"],["Membrane: hereditary spherocytosis, elliptocytosis","Immune: warm/cold AIHA, HDN, transfusion reaction, drugs"],["Enzyme: G6PD, pyruvate kinase deficiency","Mechanical: prosthetic valves, MAHA (DIC, TTP, HUS), march haemoglobinuria"],["Haemoglobin: sickle cell, thalassaemia","Infections: malaria, Clostridium"],["Acquired membrane: PNH","Hypersplenism, burns, snake venom"]]],
["Laboratory diagnosis",["Evidence of increased destruction: ↑ unconjugated bilirubin, ↑ LDH, ↓ haptoglobin, ↑ urinary urobilinogen","Intravascular: haemoglobinaemia, haemoglobinuria, haemosiderinuria","Evidence of compensation: reticulocytosis, polychromasia, nucleated RBCs, erythroid hyperplasia","Specific tests: smear (spherocytes, sickle cells, schistocytes, bite cells), osmotic fragility, Hb electrophoresis/HPLC, G6PD assay, direct Coombs test, flow cytometry CD55/CD59 (PNH)"]],
["Intravascular vs extravascular",[["Feature","Intravascular","Extravascular"],["Site","Circulation","Spleen/liver macrophages"],["Haemoglobinuria","Present","Absent"],["Splenomegaly","Absent","Present"],["Examples","PNH, G6PD crisis, mismatched transfusion","Spherocytosis, AIHA warm"]]]],
{c:"Recognising haemolysis needs three proofs — anaemia, destruction markers and marrow compensation — followed by targeted tests to find the cause."});

Z("path7",2,"p",["Sickle cell disease is caused by a single amino acid change in haemoglobin that makes red cells bend into sickles when oxygen is low.","Like jelly that hardens into rods in the cold: deoxygenated HbS polymerises and stiffens the cell.",["Point mutation: glutamic acid → valine at position 6 of β-chain (HbS).","Sickled cells block small vessels → pain crises, infarcts.","Autosplenectomy → infections by capsulated bacteria.","Sickle trait (HbAS) protects against falciparum malaria."],"Six = valine at 6; sickle cells 'stick' in vessels."],
"Sickle cell disease is an autosomal recessive haemoglobinopathy due to a point mutation in the β-globin gene substituting valine for glutamic acid at position 6, forming HbS. On deoxygenation HbS polymerises, distorting cells into sickle shapes that are rigid, haemolyse (chronic haemolytic anaemia) and occlude microvessels (vaso-occlusive crises, infarcts of bone, spleen, lung, brain). Autosplenectomy predisposes to pneumococcal, Haemophilus and Salmonella osteomyelitis. Diagnosis: sickling test with sodium metabisulphite, solubility test, Hb electrophoresis/HPLC. Heterozygous trait is protective against falciparum malaria.",
[["Pathogenesis",["β-globin gene point mutation (GAG→GTG): Glu6Val","Deoxygenated HbS polymerises into long fibres → sickling","Factors increasing sickling: hypoxia, acidosis, dehydration, high HbS, low HbF","Repeated sickling → irreversibly sickled cells, membrane damage → haemolysis","Sticky cells adhere to endothelium → microvascular occlusion"]],
["Clinical features",["Chronic haemolytic anaemia, jaundice, pigment gallstones","Vaso-occlusive (pain) crisis: hand–foot syndrome, acute chest syndrome, stroke, priapism","Aplastic crisis (parvovirus B19), sequestration crisis","Autosplenectomy; infections; Salmonella osteomyelitis","Leg ulcers, renal papillary necrosis"]],
["Laboratory",["Smear: sickle cells, target cells, Howell–Jolly bodies","Sickling test (2% sodium metabisulphite) positive","Hb electrophoresis/HPLC: HbS 80–95%, HbF raised, no HbA","Reticulocytosis"]],
["Sickle trait (HbAS)","Asymptomatic, HbS ~40%; sickling only in severe hypoxia; protects against malaria; renal concentrating defect possible."]],
{f:"Glu6Val → HbS → deoxygenation → polymer → sickle cell\n   ├ rigid → microvascular occlusion → infarcts, pain crisis\n   └ fragile → haemolysis → anaemia, jaundice",x:[["Treatment",["Hydroxyurea (↑ HbF)","Hydration, analgesia, oxygen in crisis","Penicillin prophylaxis and vaccines","Transfusion; stem cell transplant curative"]]],c:"A single amino acid substitution explains both the haemolysis and the vaso-occlusion of sickle cell disease; raising HbF is the basis of therapy."});

Z("path7",3,"p",["Thalassaemia is an inherited problem in making enough globin chains; β-thalassaemia means too few β-chains.","A factory that makes left shoes but too few right shoes: unpaired α-chains pile up and spoil the cells.",["Reduced β-chain → excess α-chains precipitate → ineffective erythropoiesis + haemolysis.","Thalassaemia major: severe anaemia from 6 months, needs transfusion.","Marrow expansion: 'hair-on-end' skull, chipmunk face.","Electrophoresis: ↑ HbF; trait has ↑ HbA2 (> 3.5%)."],"Minor = mild with high A2; Major = marrow mad, transfusion forever."],
"Thalassaemias are autosomal recessive disorders of reduced or absent synthesis of α or β globin chains. In β-thalassaemia, mutations (mostly point mutations) cause reduced (β+) or absent (β0) β-chains. Excess α-chains precipitate in red cell precursors, causing ineffective erythropoiesis and haemolysis. β-thalassaemia major presents at 6–9 months with severe microcytic hypochromic anaemia, hepatosplenomegaly, marrow expansion (chipmunk facies, hair-on-end skull X-ray) and growth retardation; it needs lifelong transfusion with iron chelation. Thalassaemia minor (trait) is mild microcytosis with HbA2 > 3.5%. Diagnosis: smear (target cells), HPLC/electrophoresis (↑HbF, ↑HbA2).",
[["Types",[["Type","Genotype","Features"],["β-thal major","β0/β0 or β+/β+","Severe anaemia, transfusion dependent"],["β-thal intermedia","Milder mutations","Moderate anaemia, not transfusion dependent"],["β-thal minor (trait)","β/β0 or β/β+","Mild microcytosis, HbA2 ↑"]]],
["Pathogenesis",["↓ β-chain → ↓ HbA → microcytic hypochromic cells","Unpaired α-chains precipitate → membrane damage → apoptosis of precursors (ineffective erythropoiesis)","Haemolysis of circulating cells in spleen","Anaemia → ↑ EPO → marrow expansion → skeletal deformities, extramedullary haematopoiesis","↑ Iron absorption + transfusions → haemosiderosis (heart, liver, endocrine)"]],
["Laboratory",["Hb 3–6 g/dL in major; MCV and MCH very low","Smear: target cells, microcytes, basophilic stippling, nucleated RBCs","HPLC: HbF 30–90% (major), HbA2 > 3.5% (trait)","Mentzer index (MCV/RBC) < 13 in trait vs > 13 in IDA","Normal or high ferritin (differentiates from IDA)"]]],
{x:[["Complications and management",["Iron overload: cardiomyopathy, cirrhosis, diabetes, hypogonadism — chelation with desferrioxamine/deferasirox","Regular transfusion to keep Hb > 9–10 g/dL","Splenectomy for hypersplenism","Stem cell transplant curative","Prevention: carrier screening, prenatal diagnosis"]]],f:"β-gene mutation → ↓ β-globin → excess α-chains\n → precipitate in erythroblasts → ineffective erythropoiesis\n → anaemia → ↑EPO → marrow expansion (skull, face)\n → ↑ iron absorption + transfusions → haemosiderosis",c:"β-thalassaemia major is a preventable disease: carrier screening and antenatal diagnosis are as important as transfusion and chelation."});

Z("path7",4,"p",["G6PD deficiency leaves red cells unable to protect themselves from oxidant damage, so they burst after certain drugs, foods or infections.","Like a house with no fire extinguisher: fine until someone lights a match (oxidant drug).",["X-linked recessive; males affected.","Triggers: primaquine, dapsone, sulphonamides, fava beans, infections.","Heinz bodies and bite cells on smear.","Protective against falciparum malaria."],"G6PD: 'Give 6 Primaquine Doses' → haemolysis."],
"G6PD deficiency is an X-linked recessive enzyme defect of the hexose monophosphate shunt, which normally generates NADPH to keep glutathione reduced. Without it, oxidant stress oxidises haemoglobin, forming precipitates (Heinz bodies) that damage the membrane; splenic macrophages remove them, producing bite cells, and acute intravascular haemolysis follows. Triggers include drugs (primaquine, dapsone, sulphonamides, nitrofurantoin), fava beans and infections. Episodes are episodic and self-limited because young cells have more enzyme. Diagnosis: G6PD assay after the episode, fluorescent spot test.",
[["Pathogenesis",["G6PD → NADPH → reduced glutathione protects Hb from oxidants","Deficiency → oxidised Hb denatures → Heinz bodies","Splenic 'pitting' → bite cells; membrane damage → intravascular haemolysis"]],
["Clinical",["Acute haemolysis 2–3 days after trigger: pallor, dark urine, jaundice","Neonatal jaundice","Favism (Mediterranean variant)","Self-limiting (G6PD A- variant)"]],
["Lab",["Smear: bite cells, blister cells, Heinz bodies (supravital stain)","Haemoglobinuria, ↑ unconjugated bilirubin","G6PD assay — false normal during crisis (retics have more enzyme); repeat after 2–3 months"]]],
{c:"Avoiding oxidant drugs and foods prevents almost all haemolytic episodes in G6PD deficiency."});

Z("path7",5,"p",["Hereditary spherocytosis is a membrane defect that makes red cells round and fragile, so the spleen destroys them.","A balloon that loses its rubber bit by bit becomes a tight small ball that cannot squeeze through narrow gaps.",["Autosomal dominant; spectrin/ankyrin defect.","Spherocytes with no central pallor, ↑ MCHC.","Increased osmotic fragility.","Splenectomy cures the anaemia."],"Spherocytes: 'Spleen spots the spheres' — splenectomy cures."],
"Hereditary spherocytosis is usually an autosomal dominant defect in red cell membrane skeleton proteins (ankyrin, spectrin, band 3, protein 4.2). Loss of membrane fragments reduces surface area, so cells become spherical, less deformable and are trapped and destroyed in the spleen (extravascular haemolysis). Features: anaemia, jaundice, splenomegaly, pigment gallstones and aplastic crises with parvovirus B19. Lab: spherocytes lacking central pallor, raised MCHC, reticulocytosis, increased osmotic fragility, negative Coombs test, EMA binding test by flow cytometry. Splenectomy corrects anaemia though spherocytes persist.",
[["Pathogenesis",["Defective vertical linkage of membrane skeleton → membrane blebs lost","↓ Surface area : volume → spherocyte","Poor deformability → splenic sequestration → extravascular haemolysis"]],
["Clinical",["Anaemia, intermittent jaundice, splenomegaly","Gallstones (bilirubin)","Aplastic crisis with parvovirus B19; haemolytic crisis with infections"]],
["Laboratory",["Spherocytes, ↑ MCHC (> 36), reticulocytosis","Osmotic fragility ↑ (incubated test more sensitive)","Eosin-5-maleimide (EMA) flow test","Direct Coombs negative (differentiates from AIHA)"]]],
{c:"Spherocytosis with a negative Coombs test and family history is diagnostic; splenectomy (after vaccination) is curative for the anaemia."});

Z("path7",6,"p",["PNH is an acquired stem-cell defect where blood cells lose their shield against complement, so complement destroys red cells.","Like soldiers who lost their identity badges: their own army (complement) attacks them.",["PIGA gene mutation → no GPI anchor → loss of CD55 and CD59.","Intravascular haemolysis, dark morning urine.","Venous thrombosis (hepatic vein — Budd–Chiari).","Diagnosis: flow cytometry for CD55/CD59; treatment eculizumab."],"PNH: 'PIGA No Halo' — no GPI shield."],
"Paroxysmal nocturnal haemoglobinuria is an acquired clonal disorder of haematopoietic stem cells caused by a somatic mutation in the X-linked PIGA gene, which is needed for glycosylphosphatidylinositol (GPI) anchor synthesis. Cells lack GPI-linked complement regulators CD55 (DAF) and CD59 (MIRL), so red cells are lysed by complement. Features: intravascular haemolysis with haemoglobinuria (classically in morning urine), haemosiderinuria, iron deficiency, thrombosis (hepatic, portal, cerebral veins) and association with aplastic anaemia and AML. Diagnosis by flow cytometry showing CD55/CD59-deficient cells; treatment with eculizumab (anti-C5).",
[["Pathogenesis",["Somatic PIGA mutation in stem cell → no GPI anchor","Loss of CD55 (DAF), CD59 (MIRL), C8-binding protein","Uncontrolled complement → MAC formation → intravascular haemolysis","Free Hb scavenges nitric oxide → thrombosis, dysphagia, erectile dysfunction"]],
["Clinical",["Haemoglobinuria, anaemia, jaundice","Venous thrombosis — commonest cause of death","Pancytopenia; link with aplastic anaemia, MDS, AML"]],
["Lab",["Flow cytometry: CD55/CD59 negative RBCs and FLAER-negative granulocytes (gold standard)","Haemosiderinuria, ↑ LDH","Ham's acid test and sucrose lysis test (older)"]]],
{c:"PNH combines haemolysis, thrombosis and marrow failure; complement inhibition has transformed its outcome."});

Z("path7",7,"p",["Autoimmune haemolytic anaemia is when the body makes antibodies against its own red cells.","Like a home security system mistakenly tagging family members as intruders.",["Warm type: IgG, at body temperature, spherocytes, spleen destroys.","Cold type: IgM, below 37 °C, agglutination, complement lysis.","Direct Coombs test positive.","Causes: idiopathic, SLE, CLL, lymphoma, drugs, Mycoplasma, EBV."],"Warm = IgG = Grows spherocytes; Cold = IgM = Makes clumps."],
"Autoimmune haemolytic anaemia (AIHA) is immune destruction of red cells by autoantibodies, confirmed by a positive direct antiglobulin (Coombs) test. Warm AIHA (most common) involves IgG antibodies active at 37 °C; coated cells are partially phagocytosed in the spleen, forming spherocytes; causes include idiopathic, SLE, CLL, lymphomas and drugs (methyldopa). Cold agglutinin disease involves IgM binding below 37 °C in peripheral circulation, causing agglutination, complement-mediated haemolysis and acrocyanosis; causes include Mycoplasma pneumoniae, infectious mononucleosis and lymphoma. Paroxysmal cold haemoglobinuria is caused by the IgG Donath–Landsteiner antibody.",
[["Types",[["Feature","Warm AIHA","Cold AIHA"],["Antibody","IgG","IgM"],["Temperature","37 °C","< 30 °C"],["Mechanism","Extravascular (splenic)","Complement, intravascular + liver"],["Smear","Spherocytes","Agglutination"],["Causes","Idiopathic, SLE, CLL, methyldopa","Mycoplasma, EBV, lymphoma"],["Treatment","Steroids, rituximab, splenectomy","Avoid cold, rituximab"]]],
["Diagnosis",["Anaemia with haemolysis markers","Direct Coombs test positive (IgG and/or C3d)","Indirect Coombs for free antibody","Cold agglutinin titre"]]],
{c:"A positive direct Coombs test separates immune haemolysis from hereditary causes; the antibody class guides therapy."});

Z("path7",8,"p",["Aplastic anaemia is marrow failure: the bone marrow becomes empty and stops making all blood cells.","Like a farm field turned to barren land — no crop of red cells, white cells or platelets.",["Pancytopenia with a hypocellular (fatty) marrow.","Causes: idiopathic (immune), drugs (chloramphenicol), benzene, radiation, viruses (hepatitis), Fanconi anaemia.","Features: anaemia, infections, bleeding; no splenomegaly.","Diagnosis: marrow biopsy (trephine)."],"Aplastic = A-plastic: no 'plastic' (growth) in marrow."],
"Aplastic anaemia is pancytopenia with hypocellular bone marrow in the absence of infiltration or fibrosis, due to suppression or injury of multipotent stem cells. Causes: idiopathic (most, T-cell–mediated), drugs (chloramphenicol, anticancer drugs, gold), chemicals (benzene), ionising radiation, viral (non-A non-B hepatitis, parvovirus), PNH and inherited Fanconi anaemia and dyskeratosis congenita. Pancytopenia (also from megaloblastic anaemia, leukaemia, myelofibrosis, hypersplenism, marrow infiltration) presents with anaemia, infections and bleeding. Diagnosis: reticulocytopenia, marrow trephine showing > 70% fat. Treatment: stem cell transplant or immunosuppression (ATG + cyclosporine).",
[["Causes of aplastic anaemia",["Idiopathic (~65%) — autoimmune T cells","Drugs: chloramphenicol, cytotoxics, carbamazepine, gold","Chemicals: benzene, insecticides","Radiation; viruses (hepatitis, EBV, parvovirus B19)","Inherited: Fanconi anaemia (DNA repair), telomerase defects"]],
["Causes of pancytopenia",[["Hypocellular marrow","Cellular marrow","Infiltrated marrow"],["Aplastic anaemia","Megaloblastic anaemia, MDS, PNH, hypersplenism","Leukaemia, myelofibrosis, metastasis, myeloma, TB, storage disorders"]]],
["Lab findings",["Pancytopenia, normocytic/macrocytic RBC, low reticulocytes","Marrow aspirate: dry/diluted tap; trephine: fat spaces > 70%, few haematopoietic cells","No splenomegaly (if present think of other causes)"]]],
{c:"Trephine biopsy is essential to diagnose aplastic anaemia and to exclude other causes of pancytopenia."});

Z("path7",9,"p",["In anaemia of chronic disease, iron is present but locked away; in sideroblastic anaemia iron enters but cannot be fitted into haem.","ACD: the warehouse is full but the doors are shut (hepcidin). Sideroblastic: material reaches the factory but the assembly line breaks.",["ACD: chronic infection, inflammation, cancer; hepcidin ↑ traps iron.","ACD: low serum iron, low TIBC, high ferritin.","Sideroblastic: ring sideroblasts in marrow (Prussian blue).","Causes: ALAS2 defect, alcohol, lead, isoniazid, MDS."],"ACD = 'Hepcidin Hides Iron'; Sideroblastic = iron 'Rings' the nucleus."],
"Anaemia of chronic disease (inflammation) is the commonest anaemia in hospital patients, seen with chronic infections, autoimmune diseases and cancer. IL-6 increases hepatic hepcidin, which blocks ferroportin, trapping iron in macrophages and reducing absorption; EPO response is also blunted. It is normocytic or mildly microcytic, with low serum iron, low TIBC and normal/raised ferritin. Sideroblastic anaemia is defective haem synthesis with iron accumulation in mitochondria of erythroblasts, seen as ring sideroblasts on Prussian blue stain; causes include X-linked ALAS2 mutation, alcohol, lead, isoniazid, copper deficiency and MDS; serum iron and ferritin are high.",
[["Comparison of microcytic anaemias",[["Test","IDA","ACD","Thal trait","Sideroblastic"],["Serum iron","↓","↓","N","↑"],["TIBC","↑","↓","N","N/↓"],["Ferritin","↓","N/↑","N","↑"],["Marrow iron","Absent","↑ in macrophages","N","Ring sideroblasts"]]],
["ACD pathogenesis",["IL-6 → ↑ hepcidin → ferroportin degraded","Iron trapped in macrophages; ↓ absorption","↓ EPO production and response; shortened RBC survival"]],
["Sideroblastic anaemia",["Hereditary: ALAS2 (X-linked), responds to pyridoxine","Acquired: MDS (RARS), alcohol, lead, isoniazid, chloramphenicol","Dimorphic smear, basophilic stippling (lead)"]]],
{c:"Ferritin and marrow iron stain separate the microcytic anaemias; treating the underlying disease is the key in ACD."});

Z("path8",0,"p",["Infectious mononucleosis ('kissing disease') is an EBV infection causing fever, sore throat, swollen glands and atypical lymphocytes.","EBV breaks into B cells; the body's T-cell police swarm out — those swollen police are the 'atypical lymphocytes'.",["Caused by Epstein–Barr virus, spread by saliva; adolescents.","Triad: fever, pharyngitis, lymphadenopathy; also splenomegaly.","Blood: lymphocytosis with atypical lymphocytes (Downey cells = CD8 T cells).","Paul–Bunnell / Monospot test positive (heterophile antibodies)."],"EBV: 'Every Boy & girl who kisses' → fever, throat, nodes."],
"Infectious mononucleosis is a benign self-limiting lymphoproliferative disease caused by Epstein–Barr virus, transmitted by saliva and affecting adolescents and young adults. EBV infects B cells via CD21; infected B cells proliferate and are controlled by cytotoxic CD8+ T cells, which appear as atypical lymphocytes. Features: fever, sore throat with exudative tonsillitis, generalised lymphadenopathy, splenomegaly, hepatitis and maculopapular rash after ampicillin. Diagnosis: absolute lymphocytosis with > 10% atypical lymphocytes, heterophile antibodies (Paul–Bunnell, Monospot), and EBV-specific antibodies (VCA IgM). Complications: splenic rupture, haemolytic anaemia, thrombocytopenia, and in immunodeficient hosts B-cell lymphoma.",
[["Pathogenesis",["EBV infects oropharyngeal epithelium then B cells via CD21 (CR2)","Latent infection → polyclonal B-cell activation → heterophile antibodies","CD8+ cytotoxic T cells proliferate = atypical lymphocytes","Lifelong latency in memory B cells"]],
["Clinical features",["Fever, malaise, sore throat, palatal petechiae","Posterior cervical lymphadenopathy, splenomegaly (50%), hepatomegaly","Rash with ampicillin/amoxicillin","Mild hepatitis"]],
["Morphology",["Blood: lymphocytosis, atypical lymphocytes — large, abundant blue cytoplasm 'ballerina skirt' indenting RBCs","Lymph node: paracortical expansion by immunoblasts (may mimic lymphoma)","Spleen: enlarged, soft, fragile — risk of rupture"]],
["Laboratory",["Heterophile antibody test: Paul–Bunnell (sheep RBC agglutination), Monospot (horse RBC)","EBV serology: VCA IgM (acute), EBNA (late)","Mildly raised transaminases"]]],
{i:"EBV is a gamma-herpesvirus with tropism for B cells; most childhood infections are silent, but primary infection in adolescence produces the IM syndrome.",x:[["Complications",["Splenic rupture — avoid contact sports for 3–4 weeks","AIHA (cold agglutinins), thrombocytopenia","Guillain–Barré, encephalitis","X-linked lymphoproliferative disease: fatal IM","EBV-associated malignancies: Burkitt lymphoma, Hodgkin lymphoma, nasopharyngeal carcinoma, post-transplant lymphoma"]]],f:"EBV (saliva) → oropharynx → B cells (CD21)\n → B-cell proliferation + heterophile Ab\n → CD8 T-cell response = atypical lymphocytes\n → fever, tonsillitis, lymphadenopathy, splenomegaly",c:"Infectious mononucleosis is diagnosed by atypical lymphocytes plus a heterophile or EBV-specific antibody test; most patients recover fully with supportive care."});

Z("path8",1,"p",["A leukaemoid reaction is a very high white cell count caused by a severe infection or stress, which looks like leukaemia but is not cancer.","Like an army mobilising in wartime — huge numbers, but all well-trained soldiers, unlike leukaemia's uncontrolled rabble.",["WBC > 50,000/µL with immature cells, due to infection, haemorrhage, haemolysis, burns or cancer.","LAP (leukocyte alkaline phosphatase) score high — low in CML.","No Philadelphia chromosome; no basophilia.","Disappears when the cause is treated."],"Leukaemoid = LAP high; CML = LAP low."],
"Leukaemoid reaction is a reactive excessive leukocytosis (usually > 50,000/µL) with immature cells in peripheral blood that mimics leukaemia. It is caused by severe bacterial infections (pneumonia, TB, sepsis), severe haemorrhage or haemolysis, burns, toxins, metastatic cancer in marrow, and drugs. Types: myeloid (most common) and lymphoid (pertussis, IM). Unlike CML it shows toxic granules, Döhle bodies, high LAP score, no basophilia, no splenomegaly, no Philadelphia chromosome, and resolves when the cause is removed.",
[["Causes",["Myeloid: severe infections (pneumococcal pneumonia, TB, sepsis), acute haemorrhage/haemolysis, burns, eclampsia, malignancy with marrow metastasis","Lymphoid: pertussis, infectious mononucleosis, chickenpox, TB"]],
["Leukaemoid reaction vs CML",[["Feature","Leukaemoid reaction","CML"],["TLC","Usually < 100,000","Often > 100,000"],["Cells","Mostly band forms, metamyelocytes","All stages; myelocyte peak"],["Toxic granules, Döhle bodies","Present","Absent"],["Basophilia, eosinophilia","Absent","Present"],["LAP score","High","Low"],["Philadelphia chromosome / BCR-ABL","Absent","Present"],["Splenomegaly","Usually absent","Massive"],["Course","Reverses with cause","Progressive"]]],
["Leukoerythroblastic reaction","Immature myeloid cells plus nucleated RBCs in blood — suggests marrow infiltration (metastasis, myelofibrosis)."]],
{c:"LAP score, cytogenetics and clinical context distinguish a leukaemoid reaction from CML."});

Z("path8",2,"p",["CML is a slow-growing cancer of the myeloid line, caused by the Philadelphia chromosome (BCR-ABL).","A car with its accelerator stuck down (BCR-ABL tyrosine kinase): myeloid cells keep multiplying, though they still mature.",["t(9;22) → BCR-ABL fusion → constant tyrosine kinase activity.","Very high WBC with all stages; myelocyte peak; basophilia.","Massive splenomegaly; low LAP score.","Phases: chronic → accelerated → blast crisis. Imatinib blocks BCR-ABL."],"CML: 'Chromosome 22 shortened (Ph), Massive spleen, Low LAP'."],
"Chronic myeloid leukaemia is a myeloproliferative neoplasm of pluripotent stem cells defined by the Philadelphia chromosome, t(9;22)(q34;q11), creating the BCR-ABL fusion gene that encodes a constitutively active tyrosine kinase. It affects adults aged 40–60 and presents with fatigue, weight loss, abdominal fullness and massive splenomegaly. Blood shows marked leukocytosis (often > 100,000/µL) with the full spectrum of myeloid cells (peaks of myelocytes and neutrophils), basophilia and eosinophilia, thrombocytosis and low LAP score. Marrow is hypercellular. The disease passes from chronic phase to accelerated phase and blast crisis (myeloid or lymphoid). Imatinib, a tyrosine kinase inhibitor, has dramatically improved survival.",
[["Pathogenesis",["Reciprocal translocation t(9;22) → short chromosome 22 = Philadelphia chromosome","BCR-ABL fusion (p210) → constitutive tyrosine kinase","Activates RAS, JAK/STAT, PI3K → proliferation, ↓ apoptosis, ↓ adhesion to stroma","Cells still mature → chronic phase"]],
["Clinical features",["Age 40–60; insidious fatigue, weight loss, sweats","Massive splenomegaly (dragging pain, splenic infarct)","Hepatomegaly; gout; bleeding or thrombosis"]],
["Peripheral blood and marrow",["TLC 50,000–500,000/µL","Full spectrum of granulocytes; bimodal peak of myelocytes and segmented neutrophils; blasts < 10% in chronic phase","Basophilia, eosinophilia; platelets normal or raised","LAP score low","Marrow: hypercellular with myeloid hyperplasia (M:E 10–20:1), increased megakaryocytes, pseudo-Gaucher cells"]],
["Phases",[["Phase","Blasts (blood/marrow)"],["Chronic","< 10%"],["Accelerated","10–19%, basophils ≥ 20%, new cytogenetic changes"],["Blast crisis","≥ 20% (2/3 myeloid, 1/3 lymphoid)"]]]],
{i:"CML is the first cancer linked to a specific chromosomal abnormality and the model for targeted therapy.",x:[["Diagnosis",["Karyotype for Ph chromosome","FISH and RT-PCR for BCR-ABL (also for monitoring)"]],["Treatment",["Imatinib, dasatinib, nilotinib (TKIs)","Allogeneic stem cell transplant in resistant cases"]]],f:"t(9;22) → BCR-ABL tyrosine kinase\n → uncontrolled myeloid proliferation (cells mature)\n → leukocytosis + basophilia + splenomegaly\n → chronic → accelerated → blast crisis",c:"CML combines a defining molecular lesion with a predictable natural history; tyrosine kinase inhibitors have converted it into a chronic manageable disease."});

Z("path8",3,"p",["AML is a fast-growing cancer where immature myeloid cells (blasts) flood the marrow and crowd out normal blood production.","Like a nursery filling with babies who never grow up and push the working adults out.",["Blasts ≥ 20% in marrow or blood.","Auer rods are characteristic (especially M3).","Symptoms from marrow failure: anaemia, infections, bleeding.","APL (M3): t(15;17), DIC, treated with all-trans retinoic acid."],"AML: 'Auer rods, Myeloblasts, Leads to marrow failure'."],
"Acute myeloid leukaemia is a neoplasm of myeloid progenitor cells with arrested maturation, producing ≥ 20% myeloblasts in marrow or blood. Mostly adults (median 65). Accumulated blasts suppress normal haematopoiesis, causing anaemia, neutropenia with infections, and thrombocytopenia with bleeding; gum hypertrophy and skin infiltration occur in monocytic types. Blasts are large with fine chromatin, nucleoli and Auer rods; MPO and Sudan black positive. FAB classifies M0–M7; WHO uses genetics (t(8;21), inv(16), t(15;17) with good prognosis). Acute promyelocytic leukaemia (M3) with t(15;17) PML-RARA presents with DIC and responds to ATRA and arsenic.",
[["Pathogenesis",["Mutations in transcription factors (RUNX1, CBF, RARA) block differentiation","Mutations in signalling (FLT3, KIT, RAS) drive proliferation","Myeloblasts accumulate → marrow failure"]],
["Clinical features",["Fatigue, pallor (anaemia)","Fever, infections (neutropenia)","Bleeding, petechiae; DIC in M3","Gum hypertrophy, skin and CNS infiltration (M4/M5)","Mild splenomegaly, bone tenderness"]],
["Lab findings",["TLC variable; blasts ≥ 20%","Blasts: large, high N:C ratio, 2–4 nucleoli, Auer rods","Cytochemistry: MPO+, Sudan black+, non-specific esterase+ (monocytic)","Flow: CD13, CD33, CD117, CD34","Cytogenetics/molecular"]],
["FAB classification",[["Type","Name","Feature"],["M0","Minimally differentiated","MPO-negative"],["M1/M2","Myeloblastic without/with maturation","M2 t(8;21)"],["M3","Acute promyelocytic","t(15;17), DIC, faggot cells"],["M4","Myelomonocytic","inv(16), eosinophils"],["M5","Monocytic","Gum, skin infiltration"],["M6","Erythroleukaemia","Erythroblasts"],["M7","Megakaryoblastic","Down syndrome, myelofibrosis"]]]],
{i:"AML accounts for most acute leukaemias in adults and needs urgent diagnosis because untreated patients die within weeks.",x:[["AML vs ALL",[["Feature","AML","ALL"],["Age","Adults","Children 2–5 y"],["Auer rods","Present","Absent"],["MPO","Positive","Negative"],["TdT","Negative","Positive"],["CNS/testis","Less","Common sanctuary"]]],["Prognosis and therapy",["Good: t(8;21), inv(16), t(15;17), NPM1","Poor: complex karyotype, FLT3-ITD, secondary AML","Induction (cytarabine + daunorubicin), consolidation, stem cell transplant; ATRA + arsenic for APL"]]],f:"Driver mutations → maturation block\n → myeloblasts ≥ 20% → crowd marrow\n → ↓ RBC (anaemia) + ↓ neutrophils (infection) + ↓ platelets (bleeding)",c:"AML is diagnosed on blast count and characterised by cytochemistry, immunophenotype and genetics, which together decide prognosis and treatment."});

Z("path8",4,"p",["Hodgkin lymphoma is a lymph-node cancer recognised by large 'owl-eye' Reed–Sternberg cells surrounded by many normal inflammatory cells.","A few bandits (RS cells) hide in a big crowd of bystanders they called in — the crowd makes up most of the tumour.",["Bimodal age: young adults and elderly.","Painless, contiguous spread of cervical lymph nodes.","B symptoms: fever (Pel–Ebstein), night sweats, weight loss.","RS cells: CD15+, CD30+. Nodular sclerosis commonest; good prognosis."],"Hodgkin: 'Owl eyes stare, 15-30 (CD), spread in a line'."],
"Hodgkin lymphoma is a B-cell lymphoid neoplasm characterised by Reed–Sternberg cells and variants in a background of reactive lymphocytes, eosinophils, plasma cells and histiocytes. It has bimodal age distribution, presents with painless cervical or mediastinal lymphadenopathy, spreads contiguously from node to node, and causes B symptoms (fever, night sweats, weight loss > 10%) and pruritus; alcohol-induced node pain is classical. Classic RS cells are large, binucleate or bilobed with prominent eosinophilic 'owl-eye' nucleoli; they express CD15 and CD30 and lack CD45. WHO types: nodular sclerosis (commonest), mixed cellularity, lymphocyte-rich, lymphocyte-depleted, and nodular lymphocyte-predominant (popcorn cells, CD20+). Staging uses Ann Arbor/Lugano; cure rates exceed 80%.",
[["Reed–Sternberg cell and variants",["Classic RS: large (15–45 µm), mirror-image nuclei, owl-eye nucleoli — CD15+, CD30+, CD45−","Lacunar cell — nodular sclerosis","Mummified cell — degenerated RS","Popcorn (L&H) cell — lymphocyte predominance, CD20+, CD15−/CD30−","Origin: germinal-centre B cell; EBV in many mixed cellularity cases"]],
["WHO classification",[["Type","Frequency","Key features"],["Nodular sclerosis","65–70%","Young women, mediastinum, collagen bands, lacunar cells; EBV−"],["Mixed cellularity","20–25%","Older men, B symptoms, many classic RS cells; EBV+"],["Lymphocyte-rich","5%","Good prognosis"],["Lymphocyte-depleted","< 5%","Elderly, HIV, worst prognosis"],["Nodular lymphocyte-predominant","5%","Popcorn cells, CD20+, excellent prognosis"]]],
["Clinical features",["Painless rubbery lymphadenopathy (cervical, mediastinal)","Contiguous spread; extranodal rare","B symptoms, Pel–Ebstein fever, pruritus","Alcohol-induced node pain","Cutaneous anergy (T-cell dysfunction)"]],
["Ann Arbor staging",[["Stage","Extent"],["I","Single node region"],["II","≥ 2 regions same side of diaphragm"],["III","Both sides of diaphragm"],["IV","Diffuse extranodal (liver, marrow)"],["Suffix","A = no B symptoms; B = B symptoms; X = bulky"]]]],
{i:"Hodgkin lymphoma is unusual among cancers in that neoplastic cells form only 1–5% of the mass; cytokines from RS cells recruit the reactive infiltrate.",x:[["Hodgkin vs non-Hodgkin",[["Feature","Hodgkin","NHL"],["Spread","Contiguous","Non-contiguous"],["Nodes","Localised, axial","Multiple, peripheral"],["Extranodal","Rare","Common"],["Mesenteric/Waldeyer","Rare","Common"],["Diagnostic cell","RS cell","Monoclonal lymphocytes"]]],["Treatment",["ABVD chemotherapy ± radiotherapy","Late effects: secondary AML, breast cancer, lung cancer, hypothyroidism, cardiac disease"]]],f:"RS cell (crippled germinal-centre B cell, ± EBV)\n → NF-κB activation → cytokines (IL-5, IL-13, TGF-β)\n → reactive infiltrate + fibrosis\n → contiguous nodal spread → B symptoms",c:"Recognition of RS cells with CD15/CD30 in the right background confirms Hodgkin lymphoma, one of the most curable malignancies."});

Z("path8",5,"p",["Burkitt lymphoma is an extremely fast-growing B-cell lymphoma driven by MYC; hairy cell leukaemia is a slow B-cell leukaemia with hair-like projections.","Burkitt: a racing car (MYC) stuck in top gear. Hairy cell: a sleepy cell with fuzzy hair that lives in the spleen.",["Burkitt: t(8;14) MYC; 'starry-sky' pattern; jaw mass in African children (EBV).","Ki-67 ~100% — fastest-growing human tumour.","Hairy cell: middle-aged men, massive splenomegaly, pancytopenia, dry tap.","Hairy cell: TRAP-positive, BRAF V600E, CD11c/CD25/CD103; responds to cladribine."],"Burkitt = 'Bursting MYC, Starry sky'; Hairy cell = 'TRAP, dry tap, big spleen'."],
"Burkitt lymphoma is a highly aggressive mature B-cell lymphoma with MYC translocation, usually t(8;14). Endemic (African) form affects children, is EBV-associated and involves the jaw; sporadic form involves ileocaecal region; HIV-associated form also occurs. Histology shows sheets of medium-sized cells with many mitoses and tingible-body macrophages giving a 'starry-sky' appearance; Ki-67 nearly 100%. Hairy cell leukaemia is an indolent B-cell neoplasm of middle-aged men with cells having fine hair-like projections, presenting with massive splenomegaly, pancytopenia and monocytopenia; marrow aspiration yields a dry tap due to reticulin fibrosis. Cells are TRAP-positive, CD11c, CD25, CD103 positive and carry BRAF V600E; purine analogues (cladribine) induce lasting remission.",
[["Burkitt lymphoma",["Genetics: t(8;14) MYC–IgH (also t(2;8), t(8;22))","Types: endemic (EBV, jaw), sporadic (abdomen, ileocaecal), immunodeficiency-associated","Morphology: diffuse sheets of monotonous cells, basophilic vacuolated cytoplasm, starry-sky pattern","Immunophenotype: CD10, CD19, CD20, BCL6+; BCL2−; Ki-67 ~100%","Rapid growth → tumour lysis syndrome; very chemosensitive"]],
["Hairy cell leukaemia",["Middle-aged men; massive splenomegaly; pancytopenia, monocytopenia","Cells: oval nuclei, pale cytoplasm with hair-like projections","Dry tap (reticulin fibrosis); 'fried egg' cells in trephine","Splenic red pulp infiltration (blood lakes)","Markers: TRAP+, CD11c, CD25, CD103, annexin A1; BRAF V600E","Treatment: cladribine, pentostatin"]],
["Comparison",[["Feature","Burkitt","Hairy cell"],["Behaviour","Highly aggressive","Indolent"],["Site","Jaw / abdomen","Spleen, marrow"],["Genetics","MYC t(8;14)","BRAF V600E"],["Hallmark","Starry sky","TRAP+, dry tap"]]]],
{c:"Both are B-cell neoplasms at opposite ends of the growth spectrum; morphology plus specific markers clinch the diagnosis."});

Z("path8",6,"p",["Multiple myeloma is a cancer of plasma cells in the bone marrow that produce a single abnormal antibody (M protein) and destroy bone.","A single rogue antibody factory taking over the marrow, flooding the blood with one product and eating the walls (bones) around it.",["CRAB: hyperCalcaemia, Renal failure, Anaemia, Bone lesions (punched-out).","M spike on serum electrophoresis; Bence Jones protein in urine.","Marrow plasma cells ≥ 10%; rouleaux on smear.","Elderly; infections due to low normal antibodies."],"Myeloma = CRAB + M spike + Bence Jones."],
"Multiple myeloma is a malignant clonal proliferation of plasma cells in the bone marrow producing a monoclonal immunoglobulin (M protein, usually IgG or IgA) and/or free light chains. It affects older adults. Neoplastic plasma cells secrete IL-6 and RANKL-activating factors that cause osteoclastic bone destruction, producing punched-out lytic lesions, pathological fractures and hypercalcaemia. Clinical features follow CRAB: hypercalcaemia, renal failure (light-chain cast nephropathy, amyloid), anaemia and bone pain; recurrent infections from suppressed normal immunoglobulins. Lab: serum M spike, urinary Bence Jones protein, rouleaux, high ESR, marrow plasmacytosis ≥ 10%, skull X-ray showing punched-out lesions.",
[["Pathogenesis",["Translocations involving IgH on 14q32 (CCND1, FGFR3)","IL-6 is a growth factor for plasma cells","Myeloma cells ↑ RANKL, ↓ OPG; secrete DKK1 (inhibits osteoblasts) → lytic lesions","Light chains filtered by kidney → casts, tubular damage"]],
["Clinical features (CRAB)",["HyperCalcaemia: confusion, constipation, polyuria","Renal failure: myeloma kidney, amyloidosis, hypercalcaemia, infection","Anaemia: marrow replacement","Bone: pain (back), lytic lesions, fractures, vertebral collapse","Infections (pneumococcus), hyperviscosity (IgA, IgM), amyloidosis (AL), neuropathy"]],
["Laboratory diagnosis",["Blood: normocytic anaemia, rouleaux, ESR > 100 mm/h, raised total protein with low albumin","Serum protein electrophoresis: M spike in γ region; immunofixation for type","Urine: Bence Jones protein (free light chains — precipitate at 40–60 °C, redissolve at 100 °C); serum free light chain ratio","Marrow: clonal plasma cells ≥ 10% (myeloma cells, Russell bodies, Dutcher bodies, flame cells)","X-ray: punched-out lesions in skull, vertebrae, pelvis; raised calcium, creatinine, β2-microglobulin"]],
["Morphology",["Bone: lytic lesions filled with red gelatinous tumour","Kidney: myeloma cast nephropathy — eosinophilic casts with giant cells around them, metastatic calcification","Plasma cells: eccentric nucleus, clock-face chromatin, perinuclear halo"]]],
{i:"Plasma cells are terminally differentiated B cells; their neoplastic counterpart retains antibody secretion, which explains both the diagnostic tests and many complications.",x:[["Related plasma cell disorders",[["Disorder","Key feature"],["MGUS","M protein < 3 g/dL, marrow plasma cells < 10%, no CRAB; 1%/year progression"],["Smouldering myeloma","M ≥ 3 g/dL or marrow ≥ 10%, no CRAB"],["Solitary plasmacytoma","Single bone/soft tissue lesion"],["Waldenström macroglobulinaemia","IgM, lymphoplasmacytic cells, hyperviscosity, no lytic lesions"]]],["Treatment",["Bortezomib, lenalidomide, dexamethasone; daratumumab","Autologous stem cell transplant","Bisphosphonates, hydration, treat hypercalcaemia"]]],f:"Clonal plasma cell (IgH translocation, IL-6)\n ├ M protein / light chains → M spike, Bence Jones, renal casts, amyloid\n ├ RANKL ↑, DKK1 → lytic lesions → bone pain, fractures, ↑Ca²⁺\n └ marrow replacement → anaemia; ↓ normal Ig → infections",c:"Myeloma is diagnosed by the triad of marrow plasmacytosis, M protein and end-organ damage (CRAB); modern drugs have greatly extended survival."});

Z("path8",7,"p",["Polycythaemia vera is a marrow cancer that makes too many red cells (and often too many white cells and platelets) on its own.","Like a factory whose 'make more' switch (JAK2) is stuck on, ignoring the boss (EPO).",["JAK2 V617F mutation in > 95%.","High Hb/haematocrit, low EPO.","Itching after hot bath, plethora, splenomegaly, thrombosis.","Treatment: venesection, aspirin, hydroxyurea."],"PV: 'JAK2 jacks up RBCs; EPO falls'."],
"Polycythaemia vera is a myeloproliferative neoplasm with EPO-independent proliferation of erythroid, granulocytic and megakaryocytic precursors, almost always due to activating JAK2 V617F mutation. It produces raised red cell mass with low serum erythropoietin (unlike secondary polycythaemia). Features: plethora, headache, dizziness, aquagenic pruritus, erythromelalgia, splenomegaly, hypertension, thrombosis (Budd–Chiari, stroke) and bleeding; may progress to spent-phase myelofibrosis or AML. Lab: Hb > 16.5 g/dL in men or > 16 in women, leukocytosis, thrombocytosis, panmyelosis in marrow, low EPO.",
[["Diagnosis (WHO major criteria)",["Hb > 16.5 g/dL (M) / > 16 (F) or haematocrit > 49%/48%","Hypercellular marrow with trilineage growth (panmyelosis)","JAK2 V617F or exon 12 mutation","Minor: subnormal serum EPO"]],
["Primary vs secondary polycythaemia",[["Feature","PV","Secondary"],["EPO","Low","High"],["WBC, platelets","Raised","Normal"],["Splenomegaly","Present","Absent"],["Cause","JAK2 mutation","Hypoxia (COPD, altitude), EPO-secreting tumours"]]],
["Complications",["Thrombosis and haemorrhage","Hyperuricaemia, gout","Myelofibrosis (spent phase), AML"]]],
{c:"Low EPO with JAK2 mutation separates PV from secondary erythrocytosis; venesection to keep haematocrit < 45% prevents thrombosis."});

Z("path8",8,"p",["Myelodysplastic syndrome is a group of marrow disorders where blood cells are made abnormally and die early, causing low counts despite a busy marrow.","A busy factory turning out mostly defective products that fail inspection.",["Elderly or after chemotherapy/radiation.","Cytopenias with hypercellular dysplastic marrow.","Dysplasia: ring sideroblasts, pseudo-Pelger cells, micromegakaryocytes.","Risk of transformation to AML; 5q– has better prognosis (lenalidomide)."],"MDS = 'Marrow Does Sloppy work' → may become AML."],
"Myelodysplastic syndromes are clonal stem cell disorders characterised by ineffective haematopoiesis, dysplasia in one or more lineages, peripheral cytopenias and risk of evolving to AML. They are seen mainly in the elderly or after alkylating agents/radiation (therapy-related). Marrow is usually hypercellular with dysplastic features: megaloblastoid erythroid precursors, ring sideroblasts, pseudo-Pelger–Huët neutrophils, hypogranular neutrophils and micromegakaryocytes; blasts < 20%. Common cytogenetic changes include 5q deletion, monosomy 7 and trisomy 8. Prognosis scored by IPSS-R.",
[["Dysplastic features",[["Lineage","Features"],["Erythroid","Megaloblastoid change, ring sideroblasts, nuclear budding"],["Granulocytic","Pseudo-Pelger–Huët (bilobed), hypogranular neutrophils"],["Megakaryocytic","Micromegakaryocytes, mononuclear forms"]]],
["WHO categories (simplified)",["MDS with single/multilineage dysplasia","MDS with ring sideroblasts (SF3B1)","MDS with isolated del(5q)","MDS with excess blasts (5–19%)"]],
["Clinical",["Anaemia (macrocytic), infections, bleeding","~30% progress to AML","Treatment: supportive transfusion, hypomethylating agents (azacitidine), lenalidomide for 5q–, stem cell transplant"]]],
{c:"MDS should be suspected in an elderly patient with unexplained macrocytic anaemia or cytopenias; marrow dysplasia and cytogenetics confirm it."});

Z("path8",9,"p",["CLL is the slow, common leukaemia of old people with many small mature-looking lymphocytes; ALL is the fast leukaemia of young children with lymphoblasts.","CLL is a crowd of old soldiers who refuse to retire; ALL is an invasion of untrained recruits.",["CLL: > 60 years, smudge cells, CD5+ CD23+ B cells, often found incidentally.","ALL: children 2–5 years, TdT+ lymphoblasts, bone pain, CNS involvement.","ALL has good prognosis in children (hyperdiploidy, t(12;21)).","CLL may transform to aggressive lymphoma (Richter)."],"CLL = Chronic, Little cells, Late age; ALL = Acute, Little kids, Lymphoblasts."],
"Chronic lymphocytic leukaemia is the commonest leukaemia of adults in the West: a clonal proliferation of mature-appearing small B lymphocytes (CD19, CD20 dim, CD5, CD23 positive) in blood (> 5000/µL), marrow and nodes. It occurs after 60, is often asymptomatic, and causes lymphadenopathy, hepatosplenomegaly, hypogammaglobulinaemia with infections and autoimmune haemolysis; smudge cells are characteristic; Richter transformation can occur. Acute lymphoblastic leukaemia is the commonest childhood cancer: lymphoblasts (TdT+, CD10, CD19 in B-ALL; CD3 in T-ALL) ≥ 20% in marrow cause marrow failure, bone pain, lymphadenopathy, CNS and testicular infiltration; T-ALL presents with a mediastinal mass. Children with ALL have > 85% cure rates.",
[["Comparison",[["Feature","CLL","ALL"],["Age","> 60 years","2–5 years (peak)"],["Cell","Small mature B lymphocyte","Lymphoblast"],["Markers","CD5, CD19, CD20, CD23","TdT, CD10, CD19 (B) / CD3, CD7 (T)"],["Smear","Smudge cells","Blasts with scant cytoplasm"],["Course","Indolent","Rapid"],["Key complications","Hypogammaglobulinaemia, AIHA, Richter transformation","CNS/testis relapse, tumour lysis"],["Prognostic markers","del(17p) poor, del(13q) good","Hyperdiploidy, t(12;21) good; t(9;22), t(4;11) poor"]]],
["CLL staging (Rai)",["0: lymphocytosis only","I: + lymphadenopathy","II: + hepatosplenomegaly","III: + anaemia (Hb < 11)","IV: + thrombocytopenia (< 100,000)"]],
["ALL clinical features",["Anaemia, fever, bleeding","Bone pain and tenderness (marrow expansion)","Lymphadenopathy, hepatosplenomegaly","Headache/vomiting (CNS), testicular enlargement","T-ALL: mediastinal mass in adolescent boys"]]],
{c:"Immunophenotyping distinguishes mature (CLL) from immature (ALL) lymphoid neoplasms and guides very different treatments."});

Z("path8",10,"p",["Non-Hodgkin lymphomas are a large family of lymph-node cancers, classified by cell type (B, T, NK) and whether they are slow-growing or aggressive.","Like a library sorted by author type (B or T cells) and by how fast the stories move.",["WHO classification by cell of origin: precursor vs mature; B vs T/NK.","Indolent: follicular lymphoma t(14;18) BCL2, small lymphocytic, MALT.","Aggressive: diffuse large B-cell (commonest), Burkitt, mantle cell t(11;14).","Spread is non-contiguous and extranodal sites common."],"NHL: 'Follicular 14;18 BCL2, Mantle 11;14 cyclin D1, Burkitt 8;14 MYC'."],
"Non-Hodgkin lymphomas are malignant neoplasms of B, T or NK lymphocytes other than Hodgkin lymphoma. The WHO classification groups them by lineage and maturity: precursor B and T lymphoblastic lymphoma; mature B-cell neoplasms (CLL/SLL, follicular lymphoma with t(14;18) BCL2, mantle cell lymphoma t(11;14) cyclin D1, marginal zone/MALT lymphoma linked to H. pylori, diffuse large B-cell lymphoma—commonest, Burkitt lymphoma t(8;14) MYC); and mature T/NK neoplasms (peripheral T-cell lymphoma, mycosis fungoides, adult T-cell leukaemia/lymphoma—HTLV-1, anaplastic large cell lymphoma ALK+, extranodal NK/T nasal type—EBV). Clinically they are divided into indolent and aggressive types.",
[["WHO classification (key entities)",[["Group","Entity","Marker/genetics"],["Precursor","B/T lymphoblastic leukaemia/lymphoma","TdT+"],["Mature B (indolent)","Follicular lymphoma","t(14;18) BCL2"],["","SLL/CLL","CD5+, CD23+"],["","MALT lymphoma","H. pylori, t(11;18)"],["Mature B (aggressive)","Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma","BCL6, BCL2; commonest"],["","Mantle cell lymphoma","t(11;14) cyclin D1, CD5+"],["","Burkitt lymphoma","t(8;14) MYC"],["Mature T/NK","Mycosis fungoides / Sézary","Cerebriform cells, skin"],["","Adult T-cell leukaemia","HTLV-1"],["","Anaplastic large cell","ALK, CD30"],["","Extranodal NK/T nasal","EBV"]]],
["Clinical behaviour",["Indolent (follicular, SLL, MALT): long survival, incurable with chemo","Aggressive (DLBCL, Burkitt): rapid, but curable with chemo (R-CHOP)","Presentation: painless lymphadenopathy, B symptoms, extranodal disease (GI, skin, CNS)"]]],
{c:"Classification of NHL combines morphology, immunophenotype and genetics; it determines both prognosis and therapy."});

Z("path8",11,"p",["Lymphadenitis is inflammation of lymph nodes; splenomegaly is an enlarged spleen. Both are clues to infection, blood disease or cancer.","Lymph nodes and spleen are police stations: when there is trouble (infection) or a takeover (cancer), they swell.",["Acute lymphadenitis: tender nodes, bacterial infection.","Chronic: follicular, paracortical, sinus histiocytosis patterns; TB gives caseating granulomas.","Massive splenomegaly: CML, myelofibrosis, kala-azar, chronic malaria, Gaucher.","Hypersplenism: cytopenias corrected by splenectomy."],"Massive spleen: 'CML, MF, Kala-azar, Malaria, Gaucher'."],
"Lymphadenitis is inflammation of lymph nodes. Acute non-specific lymphadenitis (bacterial drainage) gives tender, swollen nodes with neutrophils and possible abscess. Chronic non-specific lymphadenitis shows follicular hyperplasia (B-cell response: RA, early HIV), paracortical hyperplasia (T-cell: viral, IM, vaccines) or sinus histiocytosis (draining cancers). Specific lymphadenitis includes tuberculous (caseating granulomas, matted nodes, cold abscess), sarcoidosis, cat-scratch disease and toxoplasmosis. Splenomegaly is enlargement of the spleen; causes are infections, congestion (portal hypertension), haematological (CML, myelofibrosis, haemolytic anaemias, lymphoma), storage diseases and infiltrations. Hypersplenism is splenomegaly with cytopenias corrected by splenectomy.",
[["Patterns of reactive lymphadenitis",[["Pattern","Zone","Causes"],["Follicular hyperplasia","Cortex (B)","RA, early HIV, syphilis, toxoplasmosis"],["Paracortical hyperplasia","Paracortex (T)","Viral, IM, drugs (phenytoin), vaccination"],["Sinus histiocytosis","Sinuses","Nodes draining carcinoma"],["Granulomatous","Variable","TB, sarcoid, fungi, cat-scratch (stellate)"]]],
["Causes of splenomegaly",[["Degree","Causes"],["Massive (> 8 cm below costal margin)","CML, myelofibrosis, kala-azar, chronic malaria, Gaucher, hairy cell leukaemia, thalassaemia major"],["Moderate","Portal hypertension, lymphoma, CLL, haemolytic anaemia"],["Mild","Acute infections (typhoid, IM, septicaemia), SBE, SLE"]]],
["Hypersplenism",["Splenomegaly + cytopenia(s) + compensatory marrow hyperplasia","Corrected by splenectomy"]]],
{c:"Node biopsy patterns and spleen size give important diagnostic clues; persistent unexplained lymphadenopathy needs excision biopsy."});

Z("path9",0,"p",["ITP: the immune system destroys platelets. TTP: tiny clots form everywhere and use up platelets because a clot-trimming enzyme (ADAMTS13) is missing.","ITP is soldiers attacking their own supply trucks; TTP is traffic jams of sticky von Willebrand strings trapping trucks in tiny roads.",["ITP: anti-GPIIb/IIIa antibodies; isolated low platelets, normal coagulation; acute in children after virus, chronic in women.","Marrow in ITP: increased megakaryocytes.","TTP pentad: fever, thrombocytopenia, MAHA (schistocytes), renal and neurological signs.","TTP: low ADAMTS13; treatment plasma exchange; avoid platelet transfusion."],"ITP: 'Immune eats platelets'; TTP: 'FAT RN' — Fever, Anaemia, Thrombocytopenia, Renal, Neuro."],
"Immune thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP) is autoimmune destruction of platelets by IgG antibodies against GPIIb/IIIa or Ib/IX, with splenic removal. Acute ITP occurs in children after viral infection and is self-limiting; chronic ITP affects adult women. Features: petechiae, purpura, mucosal bleeding with isolated thrombocytopenia, normal PT/aPTT, and marrow showing increased megakaryocytes; no splenomegaly. Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP) is due to deficiency of ADAMTS13 (inherited or autoantibody), leaving ultra-large vWF multimers that cause platelet microthrombi. The pentad: fever, thrombocytopenia, microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia with schistocytes, neurological deficits and renal dysfunction; PT/aPTT normal. Treatment: urgent plasma exchange.",
[["Comparison",[["Feature","ITP","TTP"],["Mechanism","Antiplatelet antibodies","ADAMTS13 deficiency → vWF multimers"],["Platelets","Low","Low (consumed)"],["Haemolysis/schistocytes","Absent","Present (MAHA)"],["Neuro/renal","Absent","Present"],["Coagulation tests","Normal","Normal (unlike DIC)"],["Marrow","↑ Megakaryocytes","↑ Megakaryocytes"],["Treatment","Steroids, IVIG, splenectomy, TPO agonists","Plasma exchange, caplacizumab, rituximab"]]],
["Investigations",["CBC: isolated thrombocytopenia (ITP) / anaemia with schistocytes (TTP)","PT, aPTT normal in both","TTP: ↑ LDH, ↑ indirect bilirubin, ↓ haptoglobin, ADAMTS13 activity < 10%","Marrow in ITP (if atypical): many young megakaryocytes"]],
["HUS","Closely related MAHA in children after Shiga toxin E. coli O157:H7 — renal failure predominant."]],
{c:"Both cause thrombocytopenic bleeding, but TTP is a medical emergency with haemolysis and microthrombi; schistocytes on smear are the key distinguishing clue."});

Z("path9",1,"p",["Haemophilia is an inherited bleeding disease in males due to lack of a clotting factor: factor VIII (A) or factor IX (B).","Like a bucket brigade with one person missing: water (clot) never reaches the fire properly.",["X-linked recessive; males affected, females carriers.","Bleeding into joints (haemarthrosis) and muscles; delayed bleeding after surgery.","Prolonged aPTT, normal PT, normal bleeding time and platelets.","Treatment: factor replacement; emicizumab for A."],"Haemophilia A = factor 'Ate' (VIII); B = factor 9 (Christmas disease)."],
"Haemophilia A (factor VIII deficiency, 85%) and haemophilia B (factor IX deficiency, Christmas disease) are X-linked recessive coagulation disorders. They affect males and present with spontaneous or post-traumatic deep bleeding: haemarthroses causing crippling arthropathy, muscle haematomas, and prolonged bleeding after surgery or dental extraction; petechiae are absent. Severity depends on factor level (< 1% severe). Lab: prolonged aPTT corrected by mixing with normal plasma, normal PT, platelet count and bleeding time; specific factor assay confirms. Treatment: recombinant factor concentrates, desmopressin in mild A, emicizumab; complications include inhibitor antibodies and transfusion-transmitted infections.",
[["Features",[["Feature","Haemophilia A","Haemophilia B"],["Factor deficient","VIII","IX"],["Frequency","1 in 5000 males","1 in 30,000 males"],["Inheritance","X-linked recessive","X-linked recessive"],["Lab","↑ aPTT, normal PT/BT","Same"],["Treatment","Factor VIII, DDAVP, emicizumab","Factor IX"]]],
["Severity",[["Factor level","Severity","Bleeding"],["< 1%","Severe","Spontaneous haemarthrosis"],["1–5%","Moderate","After minor trauma"],["5–40%","Mild","After surgery only"]]],
["Platelet vs coagulation bleeding",[["Feature","Platelet/vascular","Coagulation factor"],["Site","Skin, mucosa","Deep — joints, muscles"],["Lesions","Petechiae, purpura","Haematomas, haemarthrosis"],["Onset after injury","Immediate","Delayed"],["Example","ITP, vWD","Haemophilia"]]]],
{c:"Prolonged aPTT with normal PT in a bleeding male suggests haemophilia; factor assays confirm the type and severity."});

Z("path9",2,"p",["Von Willebrand disease is the commonest inherited bleeding disorder, due to low or faulty von Willebrand factor (vWF), the 'glue' that sticks platelets to injured vessels.","vWF is double-sided tape: it sticks platelets to the wall and also carries factor VIII.",["Autosomal dominant mostly; both sexes.","Mucosal bleeding: nosebleeds, menorrhagia, bleeding after extraction.","Prolonged bleeding time; aPTT may be prolonged (low VIII); normal platelet count.","Ristocetin aggregation reduced; treat with desmopressin."],"vWF does 2 jobs: platelet glue + factor VIII carrier."],
"Von Willebrand disease is the most common inherited bleeding disorder, usually autosomal dominant, caused by quantitative (type 1 — commonest, type 3 — severe, absent) or qualitative (type 2) defects of von Willebrand factor. vWF mediates platelet adhesion to subendothelial collagen via GpIb and stabilises factor VIII. Patients have mucocutaneous bleeding — epistaxis, menorrhagia, gum bleeding, prolonged bleeding after cuts or dental extraction. Lab: prolonged bleeding time, normal platelet count, aPTT normal or prolonged (low factor VIII), reduced vWF antigen and activity, abnormal ristocetin-induced platelet aggregation. Desmopressin releases stored vWF; vWF-containing concentrates for severe types.",
[["Types",[["Type","Defect","Inheritance"],["1 (70%)","Partial quantitative deficiency","AD"],["2 (A, B, M, N)","Qualitative defect","AD (mostly)"],["3","Virtually absent vWF","AR; severe, like haemophilia"]]],
["Lab tests",["Bleeding time ↑, platelet count normal","aPTT ↑ in some (↓ factor VIII)","vWF antigen and ristocetin cofactor activity ↓","Ristocetin-induced platelet aggregation ↓ (except type 2B ↑)","Multimer analysis for subtypes"]],
["Treatment",["Desmopressin (type 1)","vWF/FVIII concentrate","Tranexamic acid; OC pills for menorrhagia"]]],
{c:"vWD combines a platelet-type bleeding pattern with possible aPTT prolongation because vWF both glues platelets and protects factor VIII."});

Z("path9",3,"p",["DIC is when clotting is switched on all over the body at once, using up clotting factors and platelets, so the patient both clots and bleeds.","Like a fire department sending every truck to false alarms — when a real fire starts there are no trucks left.",["Triggers: sepsis, obstetric complications (abruption, amniotic fluid embolism), trauma, cancer (APL), snake bite.","Microthrombi → organ damage; consumption → bleeding.","Lab: ↓ platelets, ↑ PT, ↑ aPTT, ↓ fibrinogen, ↑ D-dimer, schistocytes.","Treat the cause; replace platelets, FFP, cryoprecipitate."],"DIC = 'Death Is Coming' — clots and bleeds at the same time."],
"Disseminated intravascular coagulation is an acquired thrombohaemorrhagic disorder in which widespread activation of coagulation forms microthrombi throughout the microcirculation, consuming platelets and clotting factors and activating fibrinolysis. It is always secondary: obstetric complications (abruptio placentae, retained dead fetus, amniotic fluid embolism, septic abortion), sepsis (especially Gram-negative endotoxin, meningococcaemia), malignancy (acute promyelocytic leukaemia, mucin-secreting adenocarcinoma), massive trauma and burns, snake bites and incompatible transfusion. Results: tissue ischaemia (kidney, brain, lungs, adrenal) and bleeding. Lab: thrombocytopenia, prolonged PT, aPTT and thrombin time, low fibrinogen, raised D-dimer/FDP, schistocytes.",
[["Pathogenesis",["Two triggers: release of tissue factor (placenta, tumour mucin, APL granules) and widespread endothelial injury (endotoxin, TNF, immune complexes)","→ Thrombin generation → fibrin microthrombi in small vessels","→ Consumption of platelets, fibrinogen, factors V, VIII → bleeding (consumption coagulopathy)","→ Secondary fibrinolysis → FDPs inhibit platelets and fibrin polymerisation → more bleeding"]],
["Clinical and morphology",["Bleeding: oozing from venepuncture sites, petechiae, GI/GU bleeding","Microthrombi: renal cortical necrosis, ARDS, cerebral infarcts, Waterhouse–Friderichsen syndrome (adrenal haemorrhage), Sheehan syndrome","MAHA: fragmented RBCs"]],
["Laboratory",[["Test","Result"],["Platelet count","↓"],["PT, aPTT, TT","Prolonged"],["Fibrinogen","↓"],["D-dimer, FDP","↑ (key)"],["Smear","Schistocytes"]]]],
{i:"DIC is not a disease itself but a complication of serious illnesses; its mortality reflects both the underlying cause and the coagulopathy.",x:[["Acute vs chronic DIC",["Acute (sepsis, obstetric): bleeding dominates","Chronic (cancer, dead fetus): thrombosis dominates (Trousseau syndrome), mild lab changes"]],["Management",["Treat underlying cause (deliver fetus, antibiotics, ATRA for APL)","Platelets, FFP, cryoprecipitate for bleeding","Heparin in chronic DIC with thrombosis"]]],f:"Trigger (tissue factor / endothelial injury)\n → systemic thrombin → fibrin microthrombi → ischaemic organ damage\n → consumption of platelets & factors + fibrinolysis (↑ D-dimer)\n → bleeding",c:"DIC combines thrombosis and haemorrhage; raised D-dimer with low platelets and fibrinogen in the right setting confirms it, and removing the trigger is the definitive treatment."});

Z("path9",4,"p",["Blood transfusion replaces blood or its parts; it saves lives but can cause reactions, infections and overload, so blood must be matched and screened.","Like an organ transplant in liquid form — the donor's cells must match the recipient's immune 'passwords' (ABO, Rh).",["Components: packed RBC, platelets, FFP, cryoprecipitate.","Mandatory screening in India: HIV, HBV, HCV, syphilis, malaria.","Acute haemolytic reaction: ABO mismatch — fever, back pain, haemoglobinuria, shock, DIC.","Others: febrile non-haemolytic, allergic, TRALI, TACO, iron overload, infections."],"Transfusion risks: 'HAFTI' — Haemolytic, Allergic, Febrile, TRALI/TACO, Infection."],
"Blood transfusion is the transfer of whole blood or components (packed red cells, platelet concentrate, fresh frozen plasma, cryoprecipitate) to correct deficiencies. Safe transfusion needs donor selection, ABO and Rh grouping, antibody screening, cross-matching, and mandatory screening for HIV, HBV, HCV, syphilis and malaria. Complications: acute haemolytic reactions (ABO incompatibility — fever, chills, loin pain, hypotension, haemoglobinuria, DIC, renal failure), delayed haemolytic reactions, febrile non-haemolytic reactions (leucocyte antibodies), allergic and anaphylactic reactions (IgA-deficient recipients), TRALI, circulatory overload (TACO), transmission of infections, massive transfusion problems (hypocalcaemia, hyperkalaemia, hypothermia, dilutional coagulopathy) and iron overload.",
[["Components and indications",[["Component","Storage","Use"],["Packed RBC","2–6 °C, up to 42 days (SAGM)","Anaemia, blood loss"],["Platelet concentrate","20–24 °C with agitation, 5 days","Thrombocytopenia with bleeding"],["Fresh frozen plasma","−30 °C, 1 year","Multiple factor deficiency, DIC, warfarin reversal"],["Cryoprecipitate","−30 °C","Fibrinogen, VIII, vWF, XIII"]]],
["Complications",[["Type","Examples"],["Immune acute","Acute haemolytic (ABO), febrile non-haemolytic, urticaria, anaphylaxis, TRALI"],["Immune delayed","Delayed haemolysis, alloimmunisation, TA-GVHD, post-transfusion purpura"],["Non-immune","TACO, bacterial contamination, hypothermia, citrate toxicity (↓Ca²⁺), hyperkalaemia, iron overload"],["Infections","HIV, HBV, HCV, syphilis, malaria, CMV"]]],
["Management of acute haemolytic reaction",["Stop transfusion immediately; keep IV line","Check identity and recheck grouping","Maintain BP and urine output (fluids, diuretics)","Send blood/urine samples: DCT, haemoglobinuria, coagulation","Treat DIC and renal failure"]]],
{c:"Most fatal transfusion reactions are due to clerical errors; correct identification, compatibility testing and rational component use make transfusion safe."});
